= Solution
{parent=a}

Assume first that $f$ and $g$ are <integrable functions>, so that <Fubini's theorem> justifies changing the order of the two <integrals>. In the defining <Fourier transform> of the <convolution>, put $y=x-x'$:
$$
\begin{aligned}
\widetilde h(k)
&=\int_{\mathbb R}\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-ikx}f(x-x')g(x')\,dx'\,dx\\
&=\int_{\mathbb R}\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-ik(y+x')}f(y)g(x')\,dy\,dx'\\
&=\left(\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-iky}f(y)\,dy\right)\left(\int_{\mathbb R}e^{-ikx'}g(x')\,dx'\right).
\end{aligned}
$$
This proves the <convolution theorem> with the stated normalization:
$$
\boxed{\widetilde h(k)=\widetilde f(k)\widetilde g(k).}
$$

= b
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Direct evaluation of the <Fourier transform> gives, for $k\ne0$,
$$
\widetilde g(k)=\int_{-1}^1e^{-ikx}\,dx=\frac{e^{ik}-e^{-ik}}{ik}=\boxed{\frac{2\sin k}{k}}.
$$
At $k=0$ the defining <integral> is the interval length, so $\boxed{\widetilde g(0)=2}$. This also equals the continuous limit of the displayed formula.

= c
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

The <convolution> $(g*g)(x)$ is the length of the overlap of $(-1,1)$ with $(x-1,x+1)$. This overlap has length $2-|x|$ when $|x|<2$ and zero otherwise, so $u=g*g$. The <convolution theorem> now gives
$$
\boxed{\widetilde u(k)=\widetilde g(k)^2=\frac{4\sin^2 k}{k^2},\qquad \widetilde u(0)=4.}
$$
The value at zero is also the area of the <triangular function>, providing a normalization check on its <Fourier transform>.

= d
{parent=4}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

The <translation property of the Fourier transform> gives
$$
\begin{aligned}
\widetilde r(k)&=\widetilde u(k)\sum_{n=1}^4e^{ik(4n-10)}\\
&=\frac{4\sin^2k}{k^2}\left(e^{-6ik}+e^{-2ik}+e^{2ik}+e^{6ik}\right)\\
&=\boxed{\frac{16\sin^2k}{k^2}\cos(4k)\cos(2k)},\qquad \boxed{\widetilde r(0)=16}.
\end{aligned}
$$
For the remaining <integral>, the same <translation property of the Fourier transform> identifies $e^{20ik'}\widetilde g(k')$ as the <Fourier transform> of $g(x+20)$. By the product form of the <convolution theorem>, with inverse normalization $1/(2\pi)$,
$$
\int_{\mathbb R}e^{20ik'}\widetilde g(k')\widetilde r(k-k')\,dk'=2\pi\,\mathcal F\{g(x+20)r(x)\}(k).
$$
But $g(x+20)$ is supported in $[-21,-19]$, whereas the four translated <triangular functions> making up $r$ are all supported in $[-8,8]$. Their product is identically zero. Therefore the requested <integral> is $\boxed{0}$ for every real $k$.

